@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+---
+status: proposed
+---
+
+# A mirrored skill ships no command wrapper; the installer owns model-invocability
+
+## Context
+
+A command wrapper is a five-line file that says "read
+`.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the complete workflow". It exists because
+an agent cannot natively discover a skill that lives only in `.agents/skills/` —
+the wrapper is the route in.
+
+`_MIRRORED_SKILLS` builds the other route: a named skill is copied to
+`.claude/skills/<name>`, where Claude discovers it without being told. Ten
+skills are mirrored; seven of them also ship a wrapper under the same name.
+
+That is one `/name` claiming two files, and Claude Code resolves the pair by a
+rule wfctl does not set. Its documentation says the skill wins. A session on
+2026-09-04 got the wrapper instead, and its `disable-model-invocation: true`
+refused the Skill tool with "Do not replicate this skill's workflow by other
+means" — while a second session the same day, on the same machine, resolved
+`worktree-handoff` to the skill and ran it. A gate that holds intermittently
+reads as a forgetful agent rather than as packaging (#170).
+
+The flag was not the mistake. It is on the wrapper so the model reaches the
+skill rather than a file that only points at one — a correct intent whose
+consequence is that the pointer, when it wins, refuses the thing it points at.
+
+## Decision
+
+A skill named in `_MIRRORED_SKILLS` ships no command wrapper. The two sets are
+disjoint, and the invariant is tested rather than remembered.
+
+A mirrored skill is reachable both ways from one file: Claude Code creates
+`/name` for a skill exactly as it does for a command, so deleting the wrapper
+costs no human path. An unmirrored skill keeps its wrapper, which is still the
+only route to it.
+
+## Owns truth
+
+`_MIRRORED_SKILLS` owns "may an agent invoke this skill without being asked?".
+Mirroring a skill *is* the answer, and there is no second place to state it.
+
+Neither of the other two candidates can hold it:
+
+- **The skill file cannot.** `disable-model-invocation` is not an Agent Skills
+  frontmatter key, so `test_every_shipped_skill_declares_only_spec_keys` rejects
+  it; and for `i-have-adhd` the file is vendored, where anything wfctl adds is
+  dropped by the next upstream pull (`vendor-upstream-skills`, `layer-model`).
+- **The wrapper cannot.** It is not the file that runs. Its flag governs a
+  pointer, and the agent's Skill tool is asking about the target — so the answer
+  it gives is about the wrong file even in the sessions where it is heard.
+
+## Considered
+
+- **Drop `disable-model-invocation` from the seven wrappers** — two files still
+  claim one `/name`, so which `description` the reader sees stays a coin flip,
+  and the collision that made the behavior load-order dependent is untouched. It
+  also does nothing for `i-have-adhd`, whose *skill* carries the flag from
+  upstream: the one case that cannot be edited is the one this fix cannot reach.
+- **Rename the wrapper** — removes the collision, and is otherwise sound. It
+  falls on cost rather than fault: it keeps a file whose whole content is "go
+  read the sibling you already have" and gives one workflow a second name, so
+  every handoff and issue that says `/fanning-out-code-review` acquires a second
+  possible referent. #149 was unblocked by a human typing the current name.
+- **Filter the wrappers out at copy time, keeping them in the source tree** —
+  the source tree would hold seven files that no layer installs, and the reason
+  would live in the installer rather than in the absence. Deleting them says the
+  same thing where a reader looks.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `/i-have-adhd` and `/conversation-response-shape` lose the two lines their
+  wrappers added past the pointer. Persistence is already in both SKILL.md
+  bodies. "Confirm activation in one line" is dropped rather than moved:
+  `conversation-response-shape` is the file it would layer into, it sits exactly
+  on its tested line ceiling, and that ceiling exists because rules decay with
+  length — an acknowledgement is not worth what would have to be cut for it.
+- `conversation-response-shape` becomes model-invocable, which its own
+  frontmatter comment has argued for since #99 and the wrapper was quietly
+  denying.
+- Mirroring a skill is now also a decision to delete its wrapper, and
+  un-mirroring one is a decision to write it back. `doctor`'s abandoned-entry
+  scan already reports the leftover under `.agents/commands/` either way.
+
+## Log
+
+- 2026-09-04  proposed    — #170; the wrapper's flag refused the skill it points at, for whichever session lost the tie
